1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,394
Welcome to Understanding Diffusion.

2
00:00:03,554 --> 00:00:06,725
This video walks through the
slide deck of the same name.

3
00:00:06,885 --> 00:00:10,639
It explains how flow models and
diffusion models generate data,

4
00:00:10,639 --> 00:00:12,309
and how they are trained.

5
00:00:12,829 --> 00:00:14,364
There are three chapters.

6
00:00:14,524 --> 00:00:19,974
Chapter one defines flow and diffusion models
with the help of differential equations.

7
00:00:20,134 --> 00:00:22,841
Chapter two constructs the training targets.

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,568
Chapter three turns those
targets into training algorithms.

9
00:00:28,188 --> 00:00:30,737
A short note on how to use this material.

10
00:00:30,897 --> 00:00:34,831
The PowerPoint file has a notes
section below some of the slides,

11
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:37,310
with exact page and figure references.

12
00:00:37,470 --> 00:00:40,059
The general references
are on the last slide.

13
00:00:40,579 --> 00:00:44,091
Some slides are marked with a
cherry in the top right corner.

14
00:00:44,251 --> 00:00:45,841
These slides are optional.

15
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:50,043
They contain derivations for viewers
who like to see the mathematics,

16
00:00:50,043 --> 00:00:53,035
and two cherries mean an
even deeper derivation.

17
00:00:53,195 --> 00:00:55,515
You can skip them without losing the thread.

18
00:00:56,035 --> 00:01:00,409
And if you find a mistake on the slides,
the author will be grateful for a note.

19
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:03,020
Chapter one.

20
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:08,449
We define flow models and diffusion models
through the lens of differential equations.

21
00:01:10,069 --> 00:01:13,653
We begin with flow models, which
are the deterministic case.

22
00:01:15,273 --> 00:01:20,065
The goal of a generative model is to
turn samples from a simple initial

23
00:01:20,065 --> 00:01:24,970
distribution, p_init, into samples
from the data distribution, p_data.

24
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:29,449
The initial distribution is
something we can sample from easily.

25
00:01:29,609 --> 00:01:33,063
Here it is a standard Gaussian,
in other words pure noise.

26
00:01:33,223 --> 00:01:38,188
The data distribution describes whatever
we want to generate, for example images.

27
00:01:38,348 --> 00:01:41,049
We only know it through
examples, the training set.

28
00:01:42,669 --> 00:01:46,848
To describe how noise is turned
into data, we need a few concepts.

29
00:01:47,008 --> 00:01:48,853
The first one is a trajectory.

30
00:01:49,013 --> 00:01:51,374
A trajectory is a function of time.

31
00:01:51,534 --> 00:01:57,230
For every time t between zero and one, it
gives a point X_t in d-dimensional space.

32
00:01:57,390 --> 00:02:01,351
Think of a particle moving through
space, as in the plot on the right.

33
00:02:02,971 --> 00:02:05,417
The second concept is a vector field.

34
00:02:05,577 --> 00:02:10,182
A vector field takes a location x
and a time t, and returns a vector.

35
00:02:10,702 --> 00:02:13,134
You can read that vector as a velocity.

36
00:02:13,294 --> 00:02:19,581
It tells a particle that sits at location x at
time t in which direction to move, and how fast.

37
00:02:19,741 --> 00:02:22,057
The arrows on the right show such a field.

38
00:02:23,677 --> 00:02:28,908
A trajectory and a vector field are tied
together by an ordinary differential

39
00:02:28,908 --> 00:02:30,923
equation, or ODE for short.

40
00:02:31,083 --> 00:02:32,566
It has two ingredients.

41
00:02:32,726 --> 00:02:36,893
First, an initial condition, which
says where the trajectory starts.

42
00:02:37,413 --> 00:02:44,839
Second, the equation itself: the time derivative
of X_t equals the vector field, evaluated at X_t.

43
00:02:44,999 --> 00:02:49,508
In words, the particle always moves
with the velocity that the vector field

44
00:02:49,508 --> 00:02:51,792
prescribes at its current position.

45
00:02:52,312 --> 00:02:55,481
You will also see this
written in differential form,

46
00:02:55,481 --> 00:02:58,342
with d X_t on the left and d t on the right.

47
00:02:58,502 --> 00:03:01,564
That notation becomes
useful once we add noise.

48
00:03:02,084 --> 00:03:06,192
Also notice that the next state
depends only on the current state.

49
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:08,121
The dynamics has no memory.

50
00:03:08,281 --> 00:03:10,298
This is the Markov property.

51
00:03:11,918 --> 00:03:15,740
For every starting point, the
ODE has its own trajectory.

52
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:19,598
The flow collects all of them into
a single function, called psi.

53
00:03:19,758 --> 00:03:25,212
It takes a starting point x_0 and a time t,
and returns the place that this starting

54
00:03:25,212 --> 00:03:27,274
point has moved to by time t.

55
00:03:27,794 --> 00:03:32,846
At time zero nothing has moved yet, so
the flow at time zero is the identity.

56
00:03:33,006 --> 00:03:36,705
And for a fixed starting point,
the flow follows the ODE.

57
00:03:37,225 --> 00:03:41,230
Intuitively, a flow warps space,
a bit like stirring a liquid.

58
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:46,994
To build intuition, try the interactive
visualization linked at the bottom of the slide.

59
00:03:48,614 --> 00:03:51,575
Here is why flows matter
for generative modeling.

60
00:03:51,735 --> 00:03:54,230
A flow does not only move single points.

61
00:03:54,390 --> 00:03:57,333
If we apply it to every
sample of a distribution,

62
00:03:57,333 --> 00:03:59,857
it moves the whole probability density.

63
00:04:00,377 --> 00:04:06,749
On the left, a noise sample X_0 is carried
by the vector field to a data sample X_1.

64
00:04:07,269 --> 00:04:09,786
On the right you see the
same thing for densities.

65
00:04:09,946 --> 00:04:15,239
The initial distribution, labeled p in
this figure, is deformed step by step,

66
00:04:15,239 --> 00:04:20,919
through intermediate distributions p_t,
into the target distribution, labeled q.

67
00:04:22,539 --> 00:04:25,838
Let us look at an example
that can be solved by hand.

68
00:04:25,998 --> 00:04:30,632
Take the linear vector field: u_t
of x equals minus theta times x,

69
00:04:30,632 --> 00:04:33,269
where theta is a positive constant.

70
00:04:33,429 --> 00:04:36,113
The velocity always
points toward the origin,

71
00:04:36,113 --> 00:04:38,739
and it is larger the further away we are.

72
00:04:39,259 --> 00:04:43,803
The claim is that the flow is
x_0 times e to the minus theta t.

73
00:04:43,963 --> 00:04:49,265
Every starting point decays exponentially
toward zero, which is what the plot in the

74
00:04:49,265 --> 00:04:51,023
lower right corner shows.

75
00:04:51,543 --> 00:04:55,534
One caution about notation:
here theta is just a constant.

76
00:04:55,694 --> 00:04:59,715
Later the same letter will denote
the parameters of a neural network.

77
00:05:01,335 --> 00:05:05,044
This is the first optional slide,
and it verifies the claim.

78
00:05:05,204 --> 00:05:07,766
A flow has to satisfy two conditions.

79
00:05:07,926 --> 00:05:09,799
First, the initial condition.

80
00:05:09,959 --> 00:05:14,316
At time zero the exponential
equals one, so we get x_0 back.

81
00:05:14,836 --> 00:05:16,408
Second, the ODE.

82
00:05:16,568 --> 00:05:20,546
We take the time derivative
of the exponential times x_0.

83
00:05:20,706 --> 00:05:24,840
The factor x_0 does not depend on
time, so it can be pulled out.

84
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:29,232
The derivative of the exponential
follows from the chain rule:

85
00:05:29,232 --> 00:05:32,222
it is minus theta times
the same exponential.

86
00:05:32,742 --> 00:05:36,705
Collecting the terms, we get
minus theta times the flow itself.

87
00:05:37,225 --> 00:05:41,669
And that is exactly the vector field,
evaluated at the current position.

88
00:05:41,829 --> 00:05:44,348
So the claimed flow solves the ODE.

89
00:05:45,968 --> 00:05:49,309
On a computer we cannot
follow an ODE exactly.

90
00:05:49,469 --> 00:05:54,038
We simulate it in small steps, and the
simplest scheme is the Euler method.

91
00:05:54,198 --> 00:06:00,098
Choose a number of steps n and a step size
h equal to one over n, and start at x_0.

92
00:06:00,618 --> 00:06:05,794
In every step, move a small distance h in
the direction that the vector field gives

93
00:06:05,794 --> 00:06:08,967
at the current point, and
advance the time by h.

94
00:06:09,487 --> 00:06:13,723
After n steps we arrive at time
one, and we return the trajectory.

95
00:06:15,343 --> 00:06:17,228
Where does the vector field come from?

96
00:06:17,388 --> 00:06:19,987
In a flow model it is a neural network.

97
00:06:20,147 --> 00:06:25,479
We want an ODE that carries p_init to
p_data, and we let a network with parameters

98
00:06:25,479 --> 00:06:28,260
theta play the role of the vector field.

99
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:32,372
The network takes a location
x and a time t as input,

100
00:06:32,372 --> 00:06:35,806
and returns a vector of
the same dimension as x.

101
00:06:35,966 --> 00:06:40,901
So for our purposes, a neural network
is simply a parameterized vector field.

102
00:06:42,521 --> 00:06:44,719
It is worth keeping two things apart.

103
00:06:44,879 --> 00:06:47,316
The neural network is the vector field.

104
00:06:47,476 --> 00:06:52,362
It gives the velocity, that is, the
change to make at a given time and place.

105
00:06:52,882 --> 00:06:54,408
The flow is something else.

106
00:06:54,568 --> 00:06:58,365
It is the map from start to
end that we obtain implicitly,

107
00:06:58,365 --> 00:07:01,490
by simulating the ODE
with that vector field.

108
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:03,322
We never write the flow down.

109
00:07:03,482 --> 00:07:04,932
We only simulate it.

110
00:07:06,552 --> 00:07:08,863
This gives the recipe for a flow model.

111
00:07:09,023 --> 00:07:12,870
Draw a random starting point from
p_init, the standard Gaussian.

112
00:07:13,030 --> 00:07:16,278
Follow the vector field
from time zero to time one.

113
00:07:16,438 --> 00:07:20,223
And we want the end point X_1 to
be distributed like the data.

114
00:07:20,743 --> 00:07:24,645
Training means finding network
parameters for which this is true.

115
00:07:24,805 --> 00:07:28,063
How to do that is the subject
of chapters two and three.

116
00:07:29,683 --> 00:07:31,336
Next come diffusion models.

117
00:07:31,496 --> 00:07:33,598
They add randomness to the dynamics.

118
00:07:35,218 --> 00:07:38,361
For that we need the notion
of a stochastic process.

119
00:07:38,521 --> 00:07:44,067
A stochastic process is a collection of
random variables X_t, indexed by time.

120
00:07:44,227 --> 00:07:49,030
One realization of it is again a
trajectory, also called a sample path.

121
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:54,217
But if we run the process a second time,
we get a different path, as in the plot.

122
00:07:54,377 --> 00:07:58,488
So we still map time to a point
in space, as we did for flows,

123
00:07:58,488 --> 00:08:00,723
but now with randomness on top.

124
00:08:02,343 --> 00:08:04,728
A diffusion model has two ingredients.

125
00:08:04,888 --> 00:08:07,775
The first is the vector field u, as before.

126
00:08:07,935 --> 00:08:12,482
In this context it is called the drift,
and it is the deterministic part.

127
00:08:13,002 --> 00:08:16,887
The second ingredient is new:
the diffusion coefficient sigma.

128
00:08:17,047 --> 00:08:23,662
It assigns a non-negative number to each time t,
and it controls how much randomness is injected.

129
00:08:25,282 --> 00:08:29,624
Together they define a stochastic
differential equation, or SDE.

130
00:08:29,784 --> 00:08:32,286
As before, there is an initial condition.

131
00:08:32,806 --> 00:08:35,424
The evolution equation now has two terms.

132
00:08:35,584 --> 00:08:39,940
The first is the ODE part: move
along the drift for a short time.

133
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:44,380
The second is noise: sigma times
an increment of Brownian motion.

134
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:47,833
Brownian motion is also
called the Wiener process,

135
00:08:47,833 --> 00:08:50,384
which is why it is written with a W.

136
00:08:52,004 --> 00:08:53,710
So what is Brownian motion?

137
00:08:53,870 --> 00:08:58,066
It is a stochastic process W
with three defining properties.

138
00:08:58,226 --> 00:08:59,901
One: it starts at zero.

139
00:09:00,421 --> 00:09:02,407
Two: its increments are Gaussian.

140
00:09:02,567 --> 00:09:09,097
The change between an earlier time s and a later
time t has mean zero and variance t minus s.

141
00:09:09,257 --> 00:09:11,550
So the variance grows linearly in time.

142
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:17,666
Three: increments over time intervals that do
not overlap are independent of each other.

143
00:09:18,186 --> 00:09:20,574
In addition, its paths are continuous.

144
00:09:20,734 --> 00:09:25,824
And as a side note, Brownian motion is a
Gaussian process with a particular kernel.

145
00:09:25,984 --> 00:09:27,668
We come back to that shortly.

146
00:09:29,288 --> 00:09:33,657
Why is an SDE written with these
d symbols instead of a derivative?

147
00:09:33,817 --> 00:09:37,914
Because Brownian motion is continuous,
but nowhere differentiable.

148
00:09:38,074 --> 00:09:40,965
Its paths are too rough
for a derivative to exist.

149
00:09:41,125 --> 00:09:44,273
So we need a formulation
that avoids derivatives.

150
00:09:44,793 --> 00:09:47,312
To get there, look at the ODE once more.

151
00:09:47,472 --> 00:09:53,310
The derivative is defined as a limit:
the change of X over a small time step h,

152
00:09:53,310 --> 00:09:55,855
divided by h, as h goes to zero.

153
00:09:56,375 --> 00:10:00,127
Geometrically, that is the
slope of the tangent at time t.

154
00:10:01,747 --> 00:10:05,605
On this optional slide we
rewrite the ODE without a limit.

155
00:10:05,765 --> 00:10:11,032
For a finite step h, the difference quotient
equals the vector field plus an error

156
00:10:11,032 --> 00:10:14,231
term, and the error
vanishes as h goes to zero.

157
00:10:14,751 --> 00:10:16,820
Multiply both sides by h.

158
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:19,313
Then add X_t to both sides.

159
00:10:19,473 --> 00:10:22,169
We arrive at the equation
marked with a star.

160
00:10:22,329 --> 00:10:26,477
The next point equals the current
point, plus h times the velocity,

161
00:10:26,477 --> 00:10:27,834
plus a small error.

162
00:10:27,994 --> 00:10:29,817
No derivative appears in it.

163
00:10:31,437 --> 00:10:34,049
The SDE is defined in the same way.

164
00:10:34,569 --> 00:10:40,292
Take the star equation and add one more
term: sigma times the Brownian increment

165
00:10:40,292 --> 00:10:42,832
between time t and time t plus h.

166
00:10:42,992 --> 00:10:46,211
That is the precise meaning of the d W term.

167
00:10:46,731 --> 00:10:52,052
One technical detail: the error term is
now random, so its convergence to zero is

168
00:10:52,052 --> 00:10:53,809
measured in mean square.

169
00:10:55,429 --> 00:10:57,932
How do we simulate such
a Brownian increment?

170
00:10:58,092 --> 00:10:59,698
We know its distribution.

171
00:10:59,858 --> 00:11:04,437
Over a step of length h, it is Gaussian
with mean zero and variance h.

172
00:11:04,957 --> 00:11:07,919
And a Gaussian is easy to
sample with a standard trick.

173
00:11:08,079 --> 00:11:11,168
Draw epsilon from a standard
normal distribution,

174
00:11:11,168 --> 00:11:13,869
and multiply it by the square root of h.

175
00:11:14,029 --> 00:11:16,647
The result has exactly the variance h.

176
00:11:17,167 --> 00:11:22,698
More generally, to sample from a Gaussian
with mean mu and standard deviation sigma,

177
00:11:22,698 --> 00:11:25,249
compute mu plus sigma times epsilon.

178
00:11:25,769 --> 00:11:28,392
This is called the reparameterization trick.

179
00:11:28,552 --> 00:11:33,310
You may know it from variational autoencoders,
and we will use it again and again.

180
00:11:34,930 --> 00:11:37,215
With that we can simulate an SDE.

181
00:11:37,375 --> 00:11:42,999
This is the Euler-Maruyama scheme, and it
is the Euler method with one extra term.

182
00:11:43,519 --> 00:11:48,871
In every step we draw fresh Gaussian noise
epsilon, scale it by the square root of

183
00:11:48,871 --> 00:11:52,918
the step size, multiply it by
the diffusion coefficient sigma,

184
00:11:52,918 --> 00:11:55,382
and add it to the usual Euler step.

185
00:11:57,002 --> 00:12:00,615
So a diffusion model does
the same job as a flow model.

186
00:12:00,775 --> 00:12:05,847
It transports p_init to p_data,
but with an SDE instead of an ODE.

187
00:12:06,367 --> 00:12:09,880
The drift is again a neural
network with parameters theta.

188
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:13,494
The new ingredient is the
diffusion coefficient sigma.

189
00:12:15,114 --> 00:12:17,047
Sigma is usually not learned.

190
00:12:17,207 --> 00:12:22,016
We prescribe it, either as a constant
or as a schedule that depends on time.

191
00:12:22,536 --> 00:12:26,942
The sampling recipe is the same
as before: start from p_init,

192
00:12:26,942 --> 00:12:30,661
follow the SDE, and arrive
at p_data at time one.

193
00:12:31,181 --> 00:12:32,246
Two remarks.

194
00:12:32,406 --> 00:12:36,986
If sigma is zero, the noise term vanishes
and we are back at the flow model.

195
00:12:37,146 --> 00:12:40,552
So a flow model is a special
case of a diffusion model.

196
00:12:40,712 --> 00:12:45,156
And a larger sigma means more
exploration, but if it is too large,

197
00:12:45,156 --> 00:12:48,299
the simulation becomes numerically unstable.

198
00:12:49,919 --> 00:12:53,475
An optional remark for those
who know Gaussian processes.

199
00:12:53,635 --> 00:12:59,226
Brownian motion is a Gaussian process whose
kernel is the minimum of the two time points.

200
00:12:59,386 --> 00:13:01,081
This is called the Wiener kernel.

201
00:13:01,601 --> 00:13:06,864
You can think of Brownian motion as the
counterpart of the Gaussian distribution for

202
00:13:06,864 --> 00:13:08,359
stochastic processes.

203
00:13:08,519 --> 00:13:13,207
It is the basic building block for
random processes with continuous paths,

204
00:13:13,207 --> 00:13:16,111
even though these paths have no derivative.

205
00:13:16,631 --> 00:13:20,470
All of this belongs to the wider
theory of Markov processes,

206
00:13:20,470 --> 00:13:23,081
which also covers processes that jump.

207
00:13:23,241 --> 00:13:24,714
We do not need those here.

208
00:13:26,334 --> 00:13:27,388
Chapter two.

209
00:13:27,548 --> 00:13:32,677
From conditional to marginal, or from single
data points to the full distribution.

210
00:13:32,837 --> 00:13:36,551
This chapter answers one question:
what should the network learn?

211
00:13:38,171 --> 00:13:39,956
We begin with vector fields.

212
00:13:41,576 --> 00:13:46,561
Neural networks are usually trained by
minimizing a squared error between the output

213
00:13:46,561 --> 00:13:48,403
of the network and a target.

214
00:13:48,563 --> 00:13:52,146
In regression or classification,
the target is the label.

215
00:13:52,666 --> 00:13:56,124
Here we would like to compare the
network with a target vector field.

216
00:13:56,284 --> 00:13:58,336
But nobody gives us such a target.

217
00:13:58,496 --> 00:14:00,999
We have data points, not velocities.

218
00:14:01,159 --> 00:14:04,327
So the first task is to
derive a training target:

219
00:14:04,327 --> 00:14:07,222
a vector field that carries noise to data.

220
00:14:08,842 --> 00:14:13,123
First some terminology, because these
terms will come up again and again.

221
00:14:13,283 --> 00:14:16,647
The marginal vector field is
what flow matching learns.

222
00:14:16,807 --> 00:14:20,350
The marginal score function is
what score matching learns.

223
00:14:20,870 --> 00:14:23,548
And each object exists in two versions.

224
00:14:23,708 --> 00:14:26,723
Conditional means: for
one single data point.

225
00:14:26,883 --> 00:14:30,968
Marginal means: across the whole
distribution of data points.

226
00:14:32,588 --> 00:14:37,077
This table summarizes the whole chapter,
and you may want to come back to it later.

227
00:14:37,237 --> 00:14:38,715
There are six objects.

228
00:14:38,875 --> 00:14:43,302
The rows are the probability path, the
vector field and the score function.

229
00:14:43,462 --> 00:14:45,933
The columns are conditional and marginal.

230
00:14:46,453 --> 00:14:48,515
Everything on the left is tractable.

231
00:14:48,675 --> 00:14:51,506
There is a closed formula
that we can evaluate.

232
00:14:52,026 --> 00:14:56,968
Everything on the right involves an
integral over the entire data distribution,

233
00:14:56,968 --> 00:14:59,212
and cannot be evaluated directly.

234
00:14:59,732 --> 00:15:03,419
The rest of the chapter goes
through these six cells one by one.

235
00:15:05,039 --> 00:15:07,099
We start with the conditional setup.

236
00:15:07,259 --> 00:15:12,553
Pick one single data point z, and ask how
to get from noise to exactly that point.

237
00:15:13,073 --> 00:15:18,660
A conditional probability path is a family
of distributions, one for each time t.

238
00:15:18,820 --> 00:15:23,718
At time zero it is the initial distribution,
the broad Gaussian on the left.

239
00:15:23,878 --> 00:15:27,788
At time one it is concentrated
entirely on the point z,

240
00:15:27,788 --> 00:15:30,598
which is called a Dirac distribution.

241
00:15:30,758 --> 00:15:32,767
In between, it interpolates.

242
00:15:34,387 --> 00:15:37,545
The standard choice is the
Gaussian probability path.

243
00:15:37,705 --> 00:15:42,959
At time t it is a Gaussian with mean alpha
times z, and standard deviation beta.

244
00:15:43,479 --> 00:15:46,221
Alpha and beta are called noise schedulers.

245
00:15:46,381 --> 00:15:51,632
They are functions of time with fixed
boundary values: alpha goes from zero to one,

246
00:15:51,632 --> 00:15:53,773
and beta goes from one to zero.

247
00:15:54,293 --> 00:15:59,601
The most common example is the linear one,
alpha equal to t and beta equal to one

248
00:15:59,601 --> 00:16:02,337
minus t, which is plotted on the right.

249
00:16:03,957 --> 00:16:06,283
Let us check that this
path does what we want.

250
00:16:06,443 --> 00:16:09,678
At time zero, alpha is zero and beta is one.

251
00:16:09,838 --> 00:16:15,467
The mean is zero and the variance is one, so we
have the standard Gaussian, which is p_init.

252
00:16:15,987 --> 00:16:18,975
At time one, alpha is one and beta is zero.

253
00:16:19,135 --> 00:16:21,691
The mean is z and the variance is zero.

254
00:16:21,851 --> 00:16:24,676
All the probability mass
sits on the point z.

255
00:16:25,196 --> 00:16:29,862
In between, the mean moves toward z
while the variance shrinks step by step.

256
00:16:31,482 --> 00:16:34,268
Now we go from one data
point to all of them.

257
00:16:34,428 --> 00:16:39,490
The marginal probability path is obtained
by averaging the conditional paths over

258
00:16:39,490 --> 00:16:41,045
the data distribution.

259
00:16:41,565 --> 00:16:46,656
In the formula: take the conditional path
for a data point z, weight it by how

260
00:16:46,656 --> 00:16:51,471
likely that z is under the data
distribution, and integrate over all z.

261
00:16:51,991 --> 00:16:54,496
Sampling from the marginal path is easy.

262
00:16:54,656 --> 00:16:59,096
Draw a data point z, then draw x from
the conditional path for that z.

263
00:16:59,256 --> 00:17:02,650
Evaluating its density is
hard, because of the integral.

264
00:17:03,170 --> 00:17:05,886
The marginal path connects noise and data.

265
00:17:06,046 --> 00:17:10,697
At time zero it equals p_init,
and at time one it equals p_data.

266
00:17:12,317 --> 00:17:16,280
Next we need a vector field that
belongs to the conditional path.

267
00:17:16,440 --> 00:17:20,175
The conditional vector field
is defined by one requirement.

268
00:17:20,695 --> 00:17:25,847
If we draw a starting point from p_init
and follow the ODE with this vector field,

269
00:17:25,847 --> 00:17:31,368
then at every time t the point must be
distributed according to the conditional path.

270
00:17:31,528 --> 00:17:35,752
In short: the conditional vector
field generates the conditional path.

271
00:17:35,912 --> 00:17:39,804
It carries the noise distribution
onto the single data point z.

272
00:17:41,424 --> 00:17:45,720
For the Gaussian path, this vector field
can be written down in closed form.

273
00:17:45,880 --> 00:17:48,791
It may look complicated,
but look at its structure.

274
00:17:49,311 --> 00:17:52,500
Alpha and beta are functions
that we chose ourselves,

275
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:55,087
and the dots are their time derivatives.

276
00:17:55,247 --> 00:17:59,068
So the two coefficients are just
numbers that depend on time.

277
00:17:59,228 --> 00:18:05,181
The conditional vector field is a weighted sum
of the data point z and the current location x.

278
00:18:06,801 --> 00:18:08,110
Here is the picture.

279
00:18:08,270 --> 00:18:11,144
The arrows are the Gaussian
conditional vector field.

280
00:18:11,304 --> 00:18:15,064
The circles are the Gaussian
conditional path at different times.

281
00:18:15,584 --> 00:18:20,300
If the samples follow the arrows, the
cloud of samples contracts in exactly the

282
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:25,257
right way, from the broad Gaussian on the
left onto the data point on the right.

283
00:18:26,877 --> 00:18:29,869
Now we do for vector fields
what we did for paths,

284
00:18:29,869 --> 00:18:32,203
and go from conditional to marginal.

285
00:18:32,363 --> 00:18:37,424
The marginal vector field is again an
average of the conditional vector fields over

286
00:18:37,424 --> 00:18:38,579
the data points.

287
00:18:38,739 --> 00:18:40,357
But the weight is different.

288
00:18:40,517 --> 00:18:42,966
It is not simply the data distribution.

289
00:18:43,486 --> 00:18:44,969
Look at the dashed box.

290
00:18:45,129 --> 00:18:50,786
In the numerator we have the likelihood of
x given z, times the prior p_data of z.

291
00:18:50,946 --> 00:18:54,204
In the denominator, the
marginal density at x.

292
00:18:54,364 --> 00:18:59,785
By Bayes' rule, this ratio is the posterior:
the probability of the data point z,

293
00:18:59,785 --> 00:19:03,043
given that we are currently
at the location x.

294
00:19:03,563 --> 00:19:07,199
This construction is known as
the marginalization trick.

295
00:19:08,819 --> 00:19:14,047
So intuitively, the marginal vector field
at a location x is a weighted average of

296
00:19:14,047 --> 00:19:19,339
the conditional vector fields of all data
points, and the weight of each data point

297
00:19:19,339 --> 00:19:21,368
is its posterior probability.

298
00:19:21,888 --> 00:19:28,005
Data points that plausibly explain the current
location x have a strong say in where to go next.

299
00:19:28,165 --> 00:19:30,375
Data points that do not are ignored.

300
00:19:30,895 --> 00:19:35,521
And here is the key property, which is
proved on the optional slides that follow.

301
00:19:35,681 --> 00:19:39,272
The marginal vector field
generates the marginal path.

302
00:19:39,432 --> 00:19:42,050
So following it carries p_init to p_data.

303
00:19:43,670 --> 00:19:45,776
Here is how to read that posterior.

304
00:19:45,936 --> 00:19:51,430
The sketch shows the two directions between
a data point z and a noisy location x.

305
00:19:51,950 --> 00:19:57,442
The likelihood goes forward: start from a data
point and ask where its noisy version lands.

306
00:19:57,602 --> 00:20:01,215
The posterior goes backward: we
are standing at some location x,

307
00:20:01,215 --> 00:20:04,555
and we ask which data points
could have brought us here.

308
00:20:05,075 --> 00:20:07,891
The marginal vector field does exactly this.

309
00:20:08,051 --> 00:20:13,316
At x, it looks back at all candidate data
points, takes the velocity that each of

310
00:20:13,316 --> 00:20:16,876
them would prescribe, and
averages those velocities.

311
00:20:17,036 --> 00:20:21,322
Each one is weighted by how plausible
that data point is, given x.

312
00:20:21,842 --> 00:20:25,233
An integral with a probability
weight is an expectation.

313
00:20:25,393 --> 00:20:30,888
So the marginal vector field is the expected
conditional vector field under the posterior.

314
00:20:32,508 --> 00:20:34,587
Here is the practical takeaway.

315
00:20:34,747 --> 00:20:38,631
We never solve the problem for the
whole data distribution directly.

316
00:20:38,791 --> 00:20:42,870
We solve it one data point at a time,
and that turns out to be enough.

317
00:20:43,390 --> 00:20:49,644
Generating single data points is a proxy
for generating the full distribution p_data.

318
00:20:49,804 --> 00:20:54,278
Keep this in mind for the practical
session, because this is exactly what the

319
00:20:54,278 --> 00:20:55,563
training code does.

320
00:20:57,183 --> 00:20:59,348
One more remark before we go on.

321
00:20:59,508 --> 00:21:04,054
There are infinitely many ways to move
probability mass from noise to data.

322
00:21:04,214 --> 00:21:08,638
Earlier generative models were trained
without saying anything about what the

323
00:21:08,638 --> 00:21:11,173
distribution should look like in between.

324
00:21:11,693 --> 00:21:17,795
A key idea of diffusion and flow models is
to fix the intermediate path p_t in advance.

325
00:21:17,955 --> 00:21:22,448
Once the path is fixed, training
becomes a simple regression problem,

326
00:21:22,448 --> 00:21:25,167
and that scales to very large datasets.

327
00:21:26,787 --> 00:21:30,229
This is one of the optional,
more mathematical slides.

328
00:21:30,389 --> 00:21:35,977
We claimed that following the marginal vector
field makes the samples follow the marginal path.

329
00:21:36,137 --> 00:21:39,372
The tool for proving this
is the continuity equation.

330
00:21:39,892 --> 00:21:44,407
Take an ODE with a random
start, X_0 drawn from p_init.

331
00:21:44,927 --> 00:21:50,245
Then the statement that X_t is distributed
according to p_t, for every time between

332
00:21:50,245 --> 00:21:54,462
zero and one, is equivalent to
a partial differential equation.

333
00:21:54,622 --> 00:22:01,172
The time derivative of the density at x
equals minus the divergence of p_t times u_t.

334
00:22:01,692 --> 00:22:06,395
At the bottom you see the definition of
the divergence: for each coordinate, take

335
00:22:06,395 --> 00:22:11,104
the partial derivative of that component
of the vector field, and add them up.

336
00:22:12,724 --> 00:22:15,470
The intuition is conservation of mass.

337
00:22:15,630 --> 00:22:18,252
Look at the small box
around x in the sketch.

338
00:22:18,412 --> 00:22:22,359
The vector field carries probability
mass into the box and out of it.

339
00:22:22,879 --> 00:22:28,162
The divergence measures the net outflow,
so minus the divergence is the net inflow.

340
00:22:28,322 --> 00:22:33,793
The continuity equation says that the
density at x changes by exactly what flows in

341
00:22:33,793 --> 00:22:35,357
minus what flows out.

342
00:22:35,517 --> 00:22:38,426
No mass is created and none is destroyed.

343
00:22:38,946 --> 00:22:44,443
The equivalence holds in both directions, and
in a moment we will use it from right to left.

344
00:22:46,063 --> 00:22:47,789
Here is the plan for the proof.

345
00:22:47,949 --> 00:22:53,069
We want to show that samples which follow
the marginal vector field are distributed

346
00:22:53,069 --> 00:22:55,408
according to the marginal path p_t.

347
00:22:55,928 --> 00:23:00,813
By the equivalence from the previous slide, it
is enough to check the continuity equation.

348
00:23:00,973 --> 00:23:06,373
So we start from the time derivative of
p_t, and we show that it equals minus the

349
00:23:06,373 --> 00:23:09,886
divergence of p_t times
the marginal vector field.

350
00:23:10,406 --> 00:23:13,652
Once that is done, the
equivalence gives us the result.

351
00:23:13,812 --> 00:23:17,168
If we go with the flow, we
follow the probability path.

352
00:23:18,788 --> 00:23:23,690
In line one, we write the marginal
density as the integral of the conditional

353
00:23:23,690 --> 00:23:26,290
density against the data distribution.

354
00:23:26,810 --> 00:23:30,667
In line two, we move the time
derivative inside the integral.

355
00:23:30,827 --> 00:23:33,815
Only the conditional
density depends on time.

356
00:23:34,335 --> 00:23:36,103
Line three is the key step.

357
00:23:36,263 --> 00:23:41,616
For each fixed data point z, the conditional
vector field generates the conditional path.

358
00:23:41,776 --> 00:23:43,553
That is how we constructed it.

359
00:23:43,713 --> 00:23:48,956
So the continuity equation holds for the
conditional objects, and we can replace the

360
00:23:48,956 --> 00:23:51,508
time derivative by minus a divergence.

361
00:23:52,028 --> 00:23:56,359
In line four, the divergence acts
on x and the integral runs over z,

362
00:23:56,359 --> 00:23:59,086
so the divergence can be pulled outside.

363
00:23:59,606 --> 00:24:03,506
In line five, we multiply
and divide by p_t of x.

364
00:24:03,666 --> 00:24:08,568
The fraction in the red box is the
posterior of z given x, by Bayes' rule.

365
00:24:09,088 --> 00:24:13,270
And the integral that remains is
exactly the marginal vector field.

366
00:24:13,430 --> 00:24:18,532
So the marginal path and the marginal vector
field satisfy the continuity equation,

367
00:24:18,532 --> 00:24:20,310
which completes the proof.

368
00:24:21,930 --> 00:24:24,565
So far everything was about vector fields.

369
00:24:24,725 --> 00:24:28,876
Now we tell the same story for a
second object, the score function.

370
00:24:29,036 --> 00:24:32,144
We need it as soon as we want
to add noise during sampling.

371
00:24:33,764 --> 00:24:36,336
The score is the gradient
of the log density.

372
00:24:36,496 --> 00:24:40,401
Whenever you see nabla log in a
diffusion paper, think: score.

373
00:24:40,921 --> 00:24:44,152
The gradient is taken with
respect to the location x,

374
00:24:44,152 --> 00:24:46,954
not with respect to the network parameters.

375
00:24:47,114 --> 00:24:50,050
We drop that subscript to
keep the notation light.

376
00:24:50,570 --> 00:24:55,498
Intuitively, the score is a vector that
points in the direction in which the density

377
00:24:55,498 --> 00:24:56,500
grows fastest.

378
00:24:56,660 --> 00:25:01,461
As with vector fields, there is a
conditional score for a single data point z,

379
00:25:01,461 --> 00:25:04,595
and a marginal score for
the whole distribution.

380
00:25:06,215 --> 00:25:11,345
The marginal score can be written in the
same way as the marginal vector field:

381
00:25:11,345 --> 00:25:16,526
an integral of the conditional score,
weighted by the posterior of z given x.

382
00:25:17,046 --> 00:25:19,821
To prove it we need one fact from calculus.

383
00:25:19,981 --> 00:25:24,366
The derivative of the logarithm of a
function is one over that function,

384
00:25:24,366 --> 00:25:26,800
times the derivative of the function.

385
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:28,421
This is the chain rule.

386
00:25:28,941 --> 00:25:35,210
Applied to the density, it says that the score
equals the gradient of p_t divided by p_t.

387
00:25:35,370 --> 00:25:41,104
Here nabla is the vector of partial derivatives
with respect to each coordinate of x.

388
00:25:42,724 --> 00:25:43,975
The proof is short.

389
00:25:44,135 --> 00:25:50,917
Start with the identity from the last slide: the
score is the gradient of p_t, divided by p_t.

390
00:25:51,437 --> 00:25:54,527
In the numerator, replace
p_t by its definition,

391
00:25:54,527 --> 00:25:57,539
the integral over the conditional densities.

392
00:25:58,059 --> 00:26:01,806
The gradient is with respect to
x and the integral is over z,

393
00:26:01,806 --> 00:26:04,750
so the gradient can move
inside the integral.

394
00:26:05,270 --> 00:26:10,642
Now use the same identity again, this time
for the conditional density and read from

395
00:26:10,642 --> 00:26:11,717
right to left.

396
00:26:11,877 --> 00:26:16,374
The gradient of the conditional density
is the conditional score times the

397
00:26:16,374 --> 00:26:17,769
conditional density.

398
00:26:18,289 --> 00:26:19,735
That gives the result.

399
00:26:19,895 --> 00:26:23,448
The marginal score is the average
of the conditional scores,

400
00:26:23,448 --> 00:26:25,168
weighted by the posterior.

401
00:26:26,788 --> 00:26:31,362
Put the two formulas next to each other, and
you see that they have the same structure.

402
00:26:31,882 --> 00:26:36,361
In both formulas the dashed box
is the posterior of z given x.

403
00:26:36,521 --> 00:26:40,610
And in both, we average a conditional
object over all data points,

404
00:26:40,610 --> 00:26:42,897
with that posterior as the weight.

405
00:26:43,417 --> 00:26:45,717
At the top it is the conditional score.

406
00:26:45,877 --> 00:26:48,749
At the bottom it is the
conditional vector field.

407
00:26:48,909 --> 00:26:52,372
So whatever we learn about one of
them carries over to the other.

408
00:26:53,992 --> 00:26:56,071
Why is the conditional score useful?

409
00:26:56,231 --> 00:26:59,018
Because we design the
conditional path ourselves,

410
00:26:59,018 --> 00:27:01,758
we can work out its
score with pen and paper.

411
00:27:02,278 --> 00:27:06,828
Take the Gaussian path with mean
alpha z and standard deviation beta.

412
00:27:06,988 --> 00:27:12,166
Write down the logarithm of the Gaussian
density and differentiate with respect to x.

413
00:27:12,326 --> 00:27:17,278
What remains is minus, x minus
alpha z, divided by beta squared.

414
00:27:17,798 --> 00:27:22,719
So the conditional score points from x back
toward the mean, and it becomes stronger

415
00:27:22,719 --> 00:27:25,242
as the noise level beta becomes smaller.

416
00:27:26,862 --> 00:27:28,943
Now the reason we care about the score.

417
00:27:29,103 --> 00:27:34,585
Suppose we have the marginal vector field,
so that the ODE follows the path p_t.

418
00:27:34,745 --> 00:27:39,922
Then we can add noise of any strength sigma
to the dynamics and still follow exactly

419
00:27:39,922 --> 00:27:42,998
the same path, as long
as we correct the drift.

420
00:27:43,518 --> 00:27:45,870
The first term is the old vector field.

421
00:27:46,390 --> 00:27:51,704
The second term is the correction: sigma
squared over two, times the marginal score.

422
00:27:52,224 --> 00:27:54,467
The last term injects Brownian noise.

423
00:27:54,627 --> 00:27:59,025
The noise spreads the samples out, and
the score term pushes them back toward

424
00:27:59,025 --> 00:28:00,575
regions of high density.

425
00:28:00,735 --> 00:28:04,010
At the level of the distribution,
the two effects cancel.

426
00:28:05,630 --> 00:28:10,550
So the score is the correction term
that turns an ODE into an SDE.

427
00:28:10,710 --> 00:28:13,258
Training a flow model gives one sampler.

428
00:28:13,418 --> 00:28:18,757
Adding noise on top gives a whole family
of samplers, one for each choice of sigma,

429
00:28:18,757 --> 00:28:22,411
and in theory they all produce
the same distributions.

430
00:28:22,931 --> 00:28:26,015
In practice many current
models are pure flow models,

431
00:28:26,015 --> 00:28:28,058
because that already works well.

432
00:28:28,218 --> 00:28:32,981
But a trained network is never perfect,
and some noise during sampling can change

433
00:28:32,981 --> 00:28:34,748
the quality of the results.

434
00:28:34,908 --> 00:28:39,544
So it can be worth trying a few noise levels
and looking at which samples you like best.

435
00:28:41,164 --> 00:28:43,766
Another optional slide, for completeness.

436
00:28:43,926 --> 00:28:49,459
For an SDE, the continuity equation is
replaced by the Fokker-Planck equation.

437
00:28:49,979 --> 00:28:54,333
It contains the same transport
term as before, plus one new term:

438
00:28:54,333 --> 00:28:58,466
sigma squared over two, times
the Laplacian of the density.

439
00:28:58,986 --> 00:29:02,878
The Laplacian is the diffusion
operator from the heat equation.

440
00:29:03,038 --> 00:29:07,689
It smooths the density out, in the same way
that heat spreads through a metal plate.

441
00:29:07,849 --> 00:29:13,236
This is the equation you would use to prove
the SDE result from the previous slides.

442
00:29:14,856 --> 00:29:15,914
Chapter three.

443
00:29:16,074 --> 00:29:17,858
We now know what the targets are.

444
00:29:18,018 --> 00:29:22,060
This chapter turns them into training
algorithms that you can implement.

445
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:25,739
Two targets give two algorithms.

446
00:29:25,899 --> 00:29:30,417
Flow matching trains a network to
approximate the marginal vector field.

447
00:29:30,577 --> 00:29:34,661
Score matching trains a network to
approximate the marginal score.

448
00:29:34,821 --> 00:29:36,471
We start with flow matching.

449
00:29:38,091 --> 00:29:43,896
The goal is to find parameters theta such that
the network is close to the marginal vector field.

450
00:29:44,416 --> 00:29:49,565
The natural loss is a mean squared error:
the expected squared distance between the

451
00:29:49,565 --> 00:29:51,607
network output and the target.

452
00:29:52,127 --> 00:29:55,233
The expectation runs
over three random draws.

453
00:29:55,393 --> 00:29:58,407
A time t, uniform between zero and one.

454
00:29:58,567 --> 00:30:03,347
A data point z from the dataset, which
in code is simply the data loader.

455
00:30:03,507 --> 00:30:07,943
And a noisy location x, drawn from
the conditional path for that z.

456
00:30:09,563 --> 00:30:13,508
This is the loss we would like to
minimize, but we cannot compute it.

457
00:30:13,668 --> 00:30:15,807
The problem is the target itself.

458
00:30:16,327 --> 00:30:20,810
The marginal vector field is an integral
over the whole data distribution,

459
00:30:20,810 --> 00:30:22,989
with the posterior as the weight.

460
00:30:23,509 --> 00:30:28,750
To evaluate it at a single location x, we
would have to loop over the entire dataset.

461
00:30:28,910 --> 00:30:33,337
With a hundred million images, and
one such loop for every training step,

462
00:30:33,337 --> 00:30:34,851
that is not realistic.

463
00:30:36,471 --> 00:30:38,412
The fix looks almost too simple.

464
00:30:38,572 --> 00:30:41,833
Replace the marginal target
by the conditional target,

465
00:30:41,833 --> 00:30:44,851
for the data point z that
we have just sampled.

466
00:30:45,011 --> 00:30:47,667
This is the conditional flow matching loss.

467
00:30:48,187 --> 00:30:52,943
It is tractable, because we have a closed
formula for the conditional vector field.

468
00:30:53,463 --> 00:30:55,519
But is it the right thing to minimize?

469
00:30:55,679 --> 00:30:59,568
The conditional vector field only
knows about a single data point,

470
00:30:59,568 --> 00:31:02,459
and we want a model of
the whole distribution.

471
00:31:04,079 --> 00:31:05,405
The answer is yes.

472
00:31:05,565 --> 00:31:11,065
The theorem says that the conditional loss
equals the marginal loss plus a constant C.

473
00:31:11,225 --> 00:31:16,048
This constant is not negative, and it does
not depend on the network parameters.

474
00:31:16,568 --> 00:31:18,298
The plot shows what that means.

475
00:31:18,458 --> 00:31:22,184
The two curves have the same
shape, and one is shifted up by C.

476
00:31:22,344 --> 00:31:24,363
The marginal loss can reach zero.

477
00:31:24,523 --> 00:31:29,305
The conditional loss generally cannot,
because the same location x can be reached

478
00:31:29,305 --> 00:31:33,499
from many different data points, so
the conditional target is noisy.

479
00:31:34,019 --> 00:31:36,566
But we do not care about
the value of the loss.

480
00:31:36,726 --> 00:31:39,997
We care about where the minimum
is, the point theta star,

481
00:31:39,997 --> 00:31:42,300
and that is the same for both curves.

482
00:31:43,920 --> 00:31:45,557
Two consequences follow.

483
00:31:45,717 --> 00:31:50,444
First, the minimizer of the conditional
loss is the marginal vector field.

484
00:31:50,604 --> 00:31:55,036
If training were perfect, simulating
the network would turn noise into data,

485
00:31:55,036 --> 00:31:56,906
which was the goal all along.

486
00:31:57,426 --> 00:32:00,860
Second, the gradients of the
two losses are identical,

487
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:03,775
because the gradient of a constant is zero.

488
00:32:04,295 --> 00:32:09,254
So stochastic gradient descent on the
conditional loss is stochastic gradient

489
00:32:09,254 --> 00:32:11,301
descent on the marginal loss.

490
00:32:11,461 --> 00:32:16,893
Individual mini-batch updates are noisy, but
on average they point in the same direction.

491
00:32:18,513 --> 00:32:20,879
This gives the general training procedure.

492
00:32:21,039 --> 00:32:26,304
For each mini-batch: sample a data point
z, sample a time t, sample a noisy point x

493
00:32:26,304 --> 00:32:31,442
from the conditional path, compute the
squared difference between the network and

494
00:32:31,442 --> 00:32:34,730
the conditional target,
and take a gradient step.

495
00:32:35,250 --> 00:32:38,557
Notice that the marginal
vector field never appears.

496
00:32:38,717 --> 00:32:40,372
It emerges implicitly.

497
00:32:40,532 --> 00:32:45,173
Over many samples, the network sees
different conditional targets at the same

498
00:32:45,173 --> 00:32:50,537
location and has to compromise between them,
and the best compromise is their average.

499
00:32:51,057 --> 00:32:54,875
The network has two inputs,
the time t and the location x.

500
00:32:55,035 --> 00:32:59,694
It regresses toward individual data
points, and in the long run it learns to

501
00:32:59,694 --> 00:33:01,529
generate the whole dataset.

502
00:33:03,149 --> 00:33:05,847
Let us make this concrete
for the Gaussian path.

503
00:33:06,007 --> 00:33:08,498
Both ingredients are design choices.

504
00:33:08,658 --> 00:33:13,877
We choose the conditional path, a Gaussian
with mean alpha z and standard deviation

505
00:33:13,877 --> 00:33:18,585
beta, and we use the matching conditional
vector field from chapter two.

506
00:33:19,105 --> 00:33:23,153
Sampling from the path is easy
with the reparameterization trick.

507
00:33:23,313 --> 00:33:29,406
Draw standard Gaussian noise epsilon, and
set x equal to alpha z plus beta epsilon.

508
00:33:29,566 --> 00:33:33,850
The small plot shows the simplest
schedule: alpha equal to t,

509
00:33:33,850 --> 00:33:36,138
and beta equal to one minus t.

510
00:33:37,758 --> 00:33:41,588
On this optional slide and the
next one, we simplify the loss.

511
00:33:41,748 --> 00:33:46,167
Start by writing the Gaussian
conditional vector field inside the norm.

512
00:33:46,687 --> 00:33:50,833
Then replace x everywhere by
alpha z plus beta epsilon.

513
00:33:50,993 --> 00:33:54,400
The expectation is now
over time, data and noise.

514
00:33:54,920 --> 00:33:56,333
Now multiply out.

515
00:33:56,493 --> 00:34:01,615
The two terms with alpha z cancel each
other, and in the last term beta cancels.

516
00:34:03,235 --> 00:34:09,236
What is left is remarkably simple: the
network evaluated at alpha z plus beta

517
00:34:09,236 --> 00:34:13,390
epsilon, minus alpha dot
z, minus beta dot epsilon.

518
00:34:13,550 --> 00:34:16,551
A dot means the derivative
with respect to time.

519
00:34:17,071 --> 00:34:21,439
So the input of the network is a
weighted combination of data and noise,

520
00:34:21,439 --> 00:34:26,535
and the regression target is the same
combination with the weights replaced by their

521
00:34:26,535 --> 00:34:27,746
time derivatives.

522
00:34:27,906 --> 00:34:32,381
That is simply the velocity of the
point x as it moves along its path.

523
00:34:34,001 --> 00:34:39,645
Now pick the simplest schedule: alpha
equals t, and beta equals one minus t.

524
00:34:39,805 --> 00:34:42,635
The time derivatives are one and minus one.

525
00:34:43,155 --> 00:34:45,110
The loss becomes very concrete.

526
00:34:45,270 --> 00:34:50,473
The input is t z plus one minus t times
epsilon, a point on the straight line

527
00:34:50,473 --> 00:34:53,626
between the noise sample and the data point.

528
00:34:53,786 --> 00:34:57,459
The target is z minus
epsilon, data minus noise.

529
00:34:57,979 --> 00:35:01,457
This is known as the conditional
optimal transport path,

530
00:35:01,457 --> 00:35:04,742
and it is a very popular
choice in current models.

531
00:35:06,362 --> 00:35:11,800
Here is the picture, taken from the
NeurIPS 2024 tutorial on flow matching.

532
00:35:11,960 --> 00:35:15,676
A noise sample on the left, an
image from the dataset on the right,

533
00:35:15,676 --> 00:35:17,686
and a straight line between them.

534
00:35:17,846 --> 00:35:20,898
At time t we have covered
a fraction t of the line.

535
00:35:21,058 --> 00:35:25,424
In our notation the noise sample
is epsilon and the image is z.

536
00:35:27,044 --> 00:35:31,208
And this is the complete training
algorithm, short enough to memorize.

537
00:35:31,368 --> 00:35:34,498
Sample a data point, a
time and a noise vector.

538
00:35:34,658 --> 00:35:36,296
Interpolate between them.

539
00:35:36,456 --> 00:35:39,202
Ask the network to predict data minus noise.

540
00:35:39,722 --> 00:35:42,866
Notice that the target is
the same at all times t.

541
00:35:43,026 --> 00:35:46,497
What changes is how much
information the input carries.

542
00:35:46,657 --> 00:35:51,620
Near time zero the input is almost pure
noise and says very little about z.

543
00:35:51,780 --> 00:35:54,849
Near time one it is almost
the clean data point.

544
00:35:55,369 --> 00:35:58,933
This is also why sampling
has to proceed step by step.

545
00:35:59,093 --> 00:36:02,727
The information is gained
gradually along the trajectory.

546
00:36:04,347 --> 00:36:06,335
Here is a physical way to see it.

547
00:36:06,495 --> 00:36:11,565
In general, the target of conditional flow
matching is a velocity, and it changes

548
00:36:11,565 --> 00:36:15,063
along the path, like on the
curved blue trajectories.

549
00:36:15,583 --> 00:36:18,153
On the straight path the
velocity is constant.

550
00:36:18,313 --> 00:36:21,385
Velocity is displacement divided by time.

551
00:36:21,545 --> 00:36:25,345
The displacement is z minus
epsilon, the time is one,

552
00:36:25,345 --> 00:36:28,034
so the velocity is z minus epsilon.

553
00:36:28,194 --> 00:36:30,653
The network learns to predict velocities.

554
00:36:32,273 --> 00:36:36,512
For those who want to see why the
two losses differ only by a constant,

555
00:36:36,512 --> 00:36:38,603
here is the proof in two slides.

556
00:36:38,763 --> 00:36:41,424
Both losses are a squared
norm of a difference.

557
00:36:41,944 --> 00:36:46,817
A squared norm of a difference expands
into three terms: the squared norm of the

558
00:36:46,817 --> 00:36:52,357
first vector, minus twice the inner product,
plus the squared norm of the second vector.

559
00:36:52,877 --> 00:36:54,769
We apply this to both losses.

560
00:36:54,929 --> 00:36:57,466
The first vector is the network output.

561
00:36:57,626 --> 00:37:03,156
The second vector is the marginal target in one
case and the conditional target in the other.

562
00:37:04,776 --> 00:37:08,217
After expanding, compare
the two losses term by term.

563
00:37:08,737 --> 00:37:12,146
The first term is the squared
norm of the network output.

564
00:37:12,306 --> 00:37:14,468
It is identical in both losses.

565
00:37:14,988 --> 00:37:18,557
The second term is the cross
term, inside the dashed box.

566
00:37:18,717 --> 00:37:23,165
One loss has the marginal target,
the other has the conditional target.

567
00:37:23,325 --> 00:37:28,462
In expectation the two are equal, because
the marginal vector field is the posterior

568
00:37:28,462 --> 00:37:30,537
average of the conditional one.

569
00:37:31,057 --> 00:37:34,551
The third term contains only
the targets, not the network,

570
00:37:34,551 --> 00:37:36,598
so it does not depend on theta.

571
00:37:37,118 --> 00:37:41,312
So the two losses can differ only
in the third term, by a constant C.

572
00:37:41,472 --> 00:37:42,727
That is the theorem.

573
00:37:44,347 --> 00:37:48,610
Once the network is trained, sampling
is the Euler method from chapter one,

574
00:37:48,610 --> 00:37:51,348
with the trained network
as the vector field.

575
00:37:51,508 --> 00:37:56,810
Draw a starting point from p_init, take
n small steps of size one over n in the

576
00:37:56,810 --> 00:38:01,017
direction the network gives,
and return the final point X_1.

577
00:38:01,537 --> 00:38:03,618
Is a flow model deterministic?

578
00:38:03,778 --> 00:38:05,967
Yes, apart from the starting point.

579
00:38:06,127 --> 00:38:11,379
Once X_0 is drawn, the trajectory is fully
determined, so there is a one-to-one map

580
00:38:11,379 --> 00:38:13,901
from the initial noise to the output.

581
00:38:15,521 --> 00:38:17,183
Now the second algorithm.

582
00:38:17,343 --> 00:38:24,639
Remember that the marginal score lets us turn the
ODE into an SDE with any diffusion coefficient.

583
00:38:25,159 --> 00:38:27,073
To use that, we need the score.

584
00:38:27,233 --> 00:38:30,928
So we train a second network,
s theta, to approximate it.

585
00:38:31,448 --> 00:38:36,479
As before, the ideal loss is a mean
squared error against the marginal score.

586
00:38:36,639 --> 00:38:43,071
And as before it is intractable, because the marginal
score is an integral over the whole dataset.

587
00:38:44,691 --> 00:38:48,701
And as before, we swap the marginal
target for the conditional one.

588
00:38:48,861 --> 00:38:53,354
You could call the result conditional
score matching, but the established name is

589
00:38:53,354 --> 00:38:54,920
denoising score matching.

590
00:38:55,440 --> 00:38:59,434
It is tractable because the
conditional score has a closed form.

591
00:38:59,594 --> 00:39:04,581
For the Gaussian path it is minus,
x minus alpha z, over beta squared.

592
00:39:06,201 --> 00:39:07,696
The same theorem holds.

593
00:39:07,856 --> 00:39:13,079
The denoising loss equals the score
matching loss plus a non-negative constant.

594
00:39:13,239 --> 00:39:18,628
So the minimizer is the marginal score, and
the gradients of the two losses are the same.

595
00:39:20,248 --> 00:39:23,208
The training loop mirrors
the one for flow matching.

596
00:39:23,368 --> 00:39:26,276
Sample a data point, a
time and a noisy point,

597
00:39:26,276 --> 00:39:28,921
and regress onto the conditional score.

598
00:39:29,081 --> 00:39:31,018
This is the general version.

599
00:39:31,178 --> 00:39:33,704
It becomes more concrete for Gaussian paths.

600
00:39:35,324 --> 00:39:38,064
Plug the Gaussian conditional
score into the loss.

601
00:39:38,584 --> 00:39:40,426
The two minus signs combine.

602
00:39:40,586 --> 00:39:44,773
We get the network plus, x minus
alpha z, over beta squared.

603
00:39:45,293 --> 00:39:49,963
Now reparameterize: x equals
alpha z plus beta epsilon.

604
00:39:50,123 --> 00:39:55,019
In the numerator alpha z cancels, and
one factor of beta cancels as well.

605
00:39:55,539 --> 00:39:58,813
The result is the network
plus epsilon over beta.

606
00:39:58,973 --> 00:40:03,712
In other words, the network has to predict
the noise that was used to corrupt the

607
00:40:03,712 --> 00:40:05,940
data point, up to a scaling factor.

608
00:40:06,100 --> 00:40:08,441
That is where the word denoising comes from.

609
00:40:10,061 --> 00:40:15,208
So the score network receives an
interpolation between a data point and noise,

610
00:40:15,208 --> 00:40:19,017
and it learns to predict
minus epsilon divided by beta.

611
00:40:19,537 --> 00:40:20,527
One warning.

612
00:40:20,687 --> 00:40:26,292
Beta goes to zero as we approach the data
at time one, and dividing by a very small

613
00:40:26,292 --> 00:40:29,444
number makes this loss numerically unstable.

614
00:40:29,604 --> 00:40:34,680
Many implementations therefore let the
network predict the noise epsilon itself and

615
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:36,634
apply the scaling afterwards.

616
00:40:38,254 --> 00:40:41,518
Here is the resulting algorithm
for the Gaussian path.

617
00:40:41,678 --> 00:40:44,648
It is the flow matching loop
with a different target.

618
00:40:44,808 --> 00:40:50,353
Sample data, time and noise, form the
noisy point, and regress the network onto

619
00:40:50,353 --> 00:40:52,218
minus epsilon over beta.

620
00:40:53,838 --> 00:40:55,579
Let us put the pieces together.

621
00:40:55,739 --> 00:40:57,237
We start from p_init.

622
00:40:57,397 --> 00:41:03,371
The exact SDE uses the true marginal
vector field and the true marginal score.

623
00:41:03,891 --> 00:41:06,208
We replace both by the trained networks.

624
00:41:06,728 --> 00:41:10,074
If training went well, the
samples follow the path p_t,

625
00:41:10,074 --> 00:41:13,357
and at time one they are
distributed like the data.

626
00:41:14,977 --> 00:41:16,722
A word on terminology.

627
00:41:16,882 --> 00:41:22,014
A denoising diffusion model is a diffusion
model with a Gaussian probability path.

628
00:41:22,174 --> 00:41:26,262
Most people drop the word denoising
and simply say diffusion model.

629
00:41:26,782 --> 00:41:30,866
These models have a special property:
the score and the vector field can be

630
00:41:30,866 --> 00:41:32,462
converted into each other.

631
00:41:32,982 --> 00:41:38,254
Many diffusion models use score matching
and rely on Gaussian conditional paths.

632
00:41:38,414 --> 00:41:42,138
For those models, a trained score
network is all that is needed.

633
00:41:43,758 --> 00:41:45,809
Here is why the conversion works.

634
00:41:45,969 --> 00:41:51,184
For the Gaussian path, the conditional
vector field is a linear combination of the

635
00:41:51,184 --> 00:41:53,399
data point z and the location x.

636
00:41:53,559 --> 00:41:55,906
The coefficients depend only on time.

637
00:41:57,526 --> 00:41:59,985
The conditional score has the same form.

638
00:42:00,145 --> 00:42:06,442
Split the fraction, and you get one coefficient
multiplying z and another one multiplying x.

639
00:42:06,602 --> 00:42:12,475
Again a linear combination of z and x, with
coefficients that depend only on time.

640
00:42:14,095 --> 00:42:18,557
The coefficients in the two formulas are
different, but that is not the point.

641
00:42:18,717 --> 00:42:21,909
The point is that both
objects are linear in x and z.

642
00:42:22,069 --> 00:42:25,857
So we can solve one formula for
z, substitute it into the other,

643
00:42:25,857 --> 00:42:28,819
and express the vector
field through the score.

644
00:42:29,339 --> 00:42:31,610
The result is the formula in the middle.

645
00:42:31,770 --> 00:42:37,212
The conditional vector field equals a
time-dependent factor times the conditional

646
00:42:37,212 --> 00:42:40,214
score, plus alpha dot over alpha, times x.

647
00:42:40,734 --> 00:42:43,038
Train one, and you get the other for free.

648
00:42:44,658 --> 00:42:48,375
The same relation holds
after marginalizing over z.

649
00:42:48,535 --> 00:42:51,773
Averaging with the posterior
is a linear operation,

650
00:42:51,773 --> 00:42:54,493
and the coefficients do not depend on z.

651
00:42:55,013 --> 00:42:57,861
For the networks this means
that one of them is enough.

652
00:42:58,021 --> 00:43:02,655
Train a score network and compute the vector
field from it, or the other way round.

653
00:43:03,175 --> 00:43:07,348
And once more: the code only ever
uses the conditional formulas.

654
00:43:07,508 --> 00:43:12,600
The marginal objects appear implicitly,
through averaging over a large dataset.

655
00:43:14,220 --> 00:43:15,826
Three closing remarks.

656
00:43:15,986 --> 00:43:17,725
First, time conventions.

657
00:43:17,885 --> 00:43:21,338
Here, noise sits at time
zero and data at time one.

658
00:43:21,498 --> 00:43:25,414
Much of the diffusion literature
uses the opposite convention,

659
00:43:25,414 --> 00:43:28,120
so check this whenever you read a paper.

660
00:43:28,640 --> 00:43:29,843
Second, history.

661
00:43:30,003 --> 00:43:35,911
Diffusion models were first described as
discrete chains of noising and denoising steps.

662
00:43:36,071 --> 00:43:40,208
This tutorial used the continuous
view with differential equations,

663
00:43:40,208 --> 00:43:43,785
which is more compact and
covers flow matching as well.

664
00:43:44,305 --> 00:43:48,363
Third, the starting distribution
does not have to be Gaussian noise.

665
00:43:48,523 --> 00:43:53,994
The framework can transport one distribution
into another: video without sound into

666
00:43:53,994 --> 00:43:59,202
video with sound, low resolution data
into high resolution data, or a partially

667
00:43:59,202 --> 00:44:01,821
hidden scene into the complete scene.

668
00:44:03,441 --> 00:44:08,940
The deck builds on the MIT course on
flow matching and diffusion models,

669
00:44:08,940 --> 00:44:14,057
in its 2025 and 2026 editions, on
the lecture notes of that course,

670
00:44:14,057 --> 00:44:18,056
and on the NeurIPS 2024
tutorial on flow matching.

671
00:44:18,216 --> 00:44:22,743
All links are on this slide, together
with a code library and an interactive

672
00:44:22,743 --> 00:44:25,305
visualization that is well worth trying.

673
00:44:26,925 --> 00:44:30,965
A fourth chapter, on conditional
generation, guidance and prompts,

674
00:44:30,965 --> 00:44:32,614
is still in preparation.

675
00:44:32,774 --> 00:44:34,236
Thank you for watching.
